  <section id="why" class="prose">
    <h2>Why own the studio</h2>
    <p>Renting a creative tool is easy until the work matters. Your prompts live in someone else's database. Your references sit behind an account you do not control. The day the vendor changes a model, raises a price or closes a plan, the thing you learned to use changes underneath you. And when you want a small change — a different export shape, a field the form does not have — you wait.</p>
    <p>The alternative is not "build your own model". Nobody here is training one. The alternative is to own the <em>workspace</em>: the projects, the saved takes, the references, the audio, the compositions, the public pages, and the code that arranges them. The models stay vendor services, chosen per job and paid per request. The studio around them is yours.</p>
    <p>That is the composition App Composer is for. One catalogue and a set of separate apps, each with its own source, data and release. Media Studio is one of those apps: an MIT-licensed Cloudflare Worker with its own D1 database, its own R2 bucket and its own deployment. It keeps working if I stop using Composer. The application, its stored projects and its media stay yours even when a vendor endpoint changes; the vendor's inference availability is a separate dependency, and this build hit that boundary for real.</p>
    <figure class="figure narrow">
      <img src={catalogue} width="1536" height="1024" alt="App Composer illustration: a catalogue hall in the centre with separate rooms for Guestbook and Showcase, each with its own data." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The catalogue-with-separate-apps model, drawn for App Composer's playbook on 2026/11/13. Media Studio joins that model as its own room, with its own storage and release. Illustration: App Composer playbook.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>There are honest trade-offs. A hosted studio is faster to start and has editors, masks and collaboration that this does not. You are the operator: you hold the Cloudflare account, the vendor keys and the release. A broken deploy is yours to fix, or your agent's. And the quality of the output is still the quality of the model you chose — owning the workspace does not make a better picture.</p>
    <p>What ownership buys is different: a stable place where the work accumulates, a bill you can read per request, and the ability to change the tool rather than request it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="prose">
    <h2>What actually works</h2>
    <p>The studio is not a mock-up with a generate button. It has a project library, real uploads, four still routes, a reference variation, real masked fill and outpaint, background removal, upscaling, two image-to-video routes, three sound routes, a browser mix with trim/gain/fades, a bounded storyboard, a true 9:16 derivative of the selected order, scoped Composer access and deliberate publication. Each claim below names what was exercised on the deployed application, and where an external route is currently blocked.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Delivered capabilities and their real state on 2026/11/14, Australia/Melbourne (AEDT, UTC+11).</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {capabilities.map(([name, detail, state]) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td>{detail}</td><td><span class:list={['tag', state.startsWith('Live') ? 'ok' : 'muted']}>{state}</span></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>"Open-source Higgsfield-style studio" describes the category: a creative workspace for images, video and sound with a library and real edits. It does not describe parity. Higgsfield's models, its camera controls, its casting and its node graph are proprietary and not reproduced here. We integrate vendor models; we do not develop, train or host foundation models, and no vendor here provides free inference.</p>
    <h3>Reused, not reinvented</h3>
    <p>The studio reuses App Composer's Get Ready, Init specification, saved brief, agent handoff and native EmDash session. This release also reuses the existing workspace/member authority: Media Studio joins Showcase and Guestbook as a granted app, with read, edit, generate, publish and admin separated. The handoff names the current actor and workspace; the studio re-checks the current grant with Composer before a privileged dispatch. It reuses Levi's MIT Fungi work where it fits: Oyster hosts a Prints installation for the earlier Composer capability run, and Darkroom's job-engine semantics informed the studio's own engine. The studio does <em>not</em> vendor Darkroom: its published catalogue only admits Fungi-gateway video models and Oyster-shaped references, which cannot serve an independent Cloudflare studio without a second job authority. That assessment is checked into the studio repository, with the reasons.</p>
    <p>What I implemented in the studio itself: the audio asset kind with real format parsing; the storyboard manifest and bounded render; signed render-input links with byte ranges; masked fill, cutout and upscale as variant assets; the browser mix; the 9:16 slate and order-preserving derivative; scoped Composer sessions; the edit-in-place composition flow; the waveform rendering; and the fixes described later.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="brief" class="prose">
    <h2>From a sentence to a specification</h2>
    <p>It starts in Composer, not in a terminal. <a href="/init?start=media-studio">Init → Media Studio</a> asks what the studio should make, which still and video models to start with, the default frame and clip length, and — after this release — the sound model and bed length. Nothing is provisioned by saving the draft. No credit is bought. No model is called.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={init} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The Init form's Media Studio section: project name, what the studio should make, still and video model, default frame and clip length, and the saving note." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The Media Studio section of Init on the live page: what the studio should make, the still and video models, and the sound model and bed length added in this release. A saved draft carries defaults, never credentials.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>The draft becomes a portable specification. The media part is small on purpose — it is a set of validated creative defaults, not a provisioning script:</p>
    <pre><code>{`"mediaStudio": {
  "version": 2,
  "focus": "both",
  "imageModel": "studio-print",
  "videoModel": "hailuo-standard",
  "audioModel": "studio-score",
  "audioSeconds": 15,
  "aspect": "portrait",
  "durationSeconds": 6,
  "projectName": "Paper & Light"
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>Revision 2 added the sound defaults. A revision 1 draft still validates: its sound fields are filled in rather than rejected, so an older saved brief does not break. The studio rechecks every value on its own server; the spec never enables a provider or promises a model the studio has not qualified.</p>
    <p>Export the spec, import it somewhere else, and the same choices come back. Then "Copy agent handoff" gives your chosen agent the pinned source, the storage and secret names, the build plan, and the human-only actions — creating accounts, signing in, approving spend. The handoff is a contract, not proof of execution. What proved it was the deployment at the end of it.</p>
    <p><a class="inline-cta" href="/media-studio/tutorial#brief">The tutorial walks the same step with the screens and checkpoints →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="build" class="prose">
    <h2>How the agent builds it</h2>
    <p>Get Ready checks the local toolchain and the accounts the agent will need. Init's handoff pins the studio repository and an exact commit, then names the owner-controlled resources: a D1 database bound as <code>DB</code>, an R2 bucket bound as <code>MEDIA</code>, a Workers AI binding named <code>AI</code>, the owner passphrase and session secret, and an optional fal.ai key. The agent creates the owner's copy, applies the migrations in order, deploys, and reads back the live source identity.</p>
    <p>The studio's release script refuses to run from a dirty tree, from a branch that is not <code>main</code>, or from a commit that is not pushed. It runs the focused tests once, deploys, then fetches <code>/healthz</code> and fails the release unless the live revision equals the commit it just shipped. That is the difference between "deployed" and "I think it deployed".</p>
    <p>Two engineering decisions shaped the build more than any other.</p>
    <h3>One job authority</h3>
    <p>Every paid request — still, clip, sound, assembly — becomes one row before anything is sent. A client request id can only create one job. An atomic SQL statement is the only budget authority, so two simultaneous requests cannot oversubscribe the month. Provider bytes are retained in R2 before the library record exists, so a failed save is retried without a second inference call. An ambiguous provider outcome keeps its reservation and is never resubmitted automatically.</p>
    <h3>Bounded rendering</h3>
    <p>Assembly is not "run ffmpeg on whatever the user selected". A composition takes two to six saved clips, each at most 10.5 seconds, 60 seconds in total, plus one saved audio bed. The render is one provider composition, and the result is copied into the studio's own bucket. The same durable job engine drives it, so a closed browser does not lose the render and a failed read does not silently re-render.</p>
    <p>The provider needs to read the participating files, and the studio's media endpoints require a current session. Rather than making a project public, the server signs one asset for one project with an expiry:</p>
    <pre><code>{`// src/lib/render-links.mjs — one asset, one project, thirty minutes
const payload = \`\${assetId}.\${projectId}.\${expires}\`;
const signature = await hmac(env.SESSION_SECRET, payload);
const url = new URL(\`/render-input/\${assetId}\`, origin);
url.searchParams.set("p", projectId);
url.searchParams.set("e", String(expires));
url.searchParams.set("s", signature);`}</code></pre>
    <p>That link lists nothing, grants no session, carries no provider key, and expires on its own. The interface says so before a render starts, because sending your clips to a renderer is a real disclosure even when the link is narrow.</p>
    <p>The assembled frame follows the first keyframe. That single detail is why the portrait cut is built by ordering portrait takes first rather than by asking a merge endpoint to re-frame one file — a path that looked reasonable and failed on the first real request, which the next section does not hide. It was also the defect: sorting by orientation overrode the shot order you chose, and that earlier portrait cut produced 4:5, not 9:16.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="use" class="prose">
    <h2>Making something real</h2>
    <p>The studio opens from the Composer menu with the existing EmDash sign-in. There is no second password in the normal path, and the browser never holds the studio's owner passphrase or the provider keys. The entry names the current actor, one workspace and the selected project; read, edit, generate, publish and admin are separate grants. Standalone sign-in still exists, because the studio is a normal independent app; it is just not the way you arrive from Composer.</p>
    <h3>A mask, not a prompt pretending to be one</h3>
    <p>The first version said masked editing was not built. It is built now, through FLUX.1 Fill on fal. Pick a still, paint the area to change, write what belongs there, and the studio uploads the source and a same-size mask as separate assets. The original stays; the fill is saved as a variant with its own hash and a parent link. The mask is real white-on-black pixels, not a sentence asking a model to “keep the rest the same”.</p>
    <p>Outpainting uses the same tool deliberately: the studio builds a 25% larger canvas, centres the original, and masks the new border. Background removal is BiRefNet returning a transparent PNG; upscaling is Clarity at 2×. All three were exercised on the deployed studio, and all three save the result beside the source rather than over it.</p>
    <p>One implementation detail was not obvious: the provider's image fetcher refused the studio's signed HTTPS links, even though they were public and worked in a browser. The fix was to send the exact bytes as a data URI. That is also why the fill job now stores the source and mask hashes on the generation row: the provider never needs a durable public copy of either.</p>
    <h3>Sound as an asset, not a soundtrack</h3>
    <p>Sound is the part most "AI video" tools skip. Here it is a first-class saved asset: WAV, MP3 or M4A uploads, or two generated routes — Studio Score for an instrumental bed of 10 to 30 seconds, Studio Voice for narration from your own script. The studio reads the real duration, sample rate and channel count from the bytes, records provenance and cost, and draws the waveform from the decoded audio rather than an illustration of one.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={sound} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Create tab with Make sound selected: Studio Score, a written direction, a 17-second length, and an estimate before the request." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>One paid request, estimated first. The length control arrived with the change below, so a bed can fit the cut instead of being padded afterwards.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>That matters for the edit. A 30-second bed under a 17-second cut produces a container with thirteen seconds of black and music — technically playable, visibly wrong. Choosing a 17-second bed produced a 17.02-second master with a 16.79-second picture: three takes, one bed, no tail.</p>
    <h3>The storyboard is the edit</h3>
    <p>A storyboard is an ordered manifest of saved takes plus the soundtrack. The interface shows the running duration, refuses a cut under two takes or over sixty seconds, and estimates the render before it is sent. Saved compositions stay editable; a render copies the manifest into the job, so editing the storyboard later never rewrites what an earlier render used.</p>
    <h3>The mix is a real mix</h3>
    <p>Music, narration and effects are separate saved assets. A composition can take up to four of them, with a role, start offset, trim in/out, gain and fades. The browser decodes the saved bytes and renders the mix deterministically with the Web Audio API, then uploads the result as a new WAV. The separate tracks and their settings stay in the manifest, so the mix can be rebuilt; the render uses the saved WAV and never claims the compose endpoint applied gain it does not support.</p>
    <h3>The 9:16 cut, after the bug</h3>
    <p>The previous release called a 768×960 file 9:16. It was 4:5, and the portrait-first sort changed the edit to suit the frame. Both were defects, and the claim was withdrawn. The replacement has two parts. The studio composes a deliberate 1080×1920 slate, generates one 720×1280 vertical slate video with Wan 2.5, and keeps those bytes so later renders reuse them. The selected takes then follow in the order chosen, composed into the slate's frame with the saved sound. No shot is re-sorted, stretched or silently regenerated.</p>
    <p>The current fal account cannot read the result of a new `ffmpeg-api` compose job: the submit is accepted, the status says completed, and the result route returns 403 with `&#123;"detail":"Access denied: You do not have permission to use this endpoint"&#125;` for the exact payload that worked earlier. That is a provider permission decision on result retrieval, not a funding problem and not a layout fix. The studio now records it as `provider_permission`, keeps the provider request id and offers Reconcile, which re-reads the same result without submitting another paid job. The in-product path is coded and unit-tested, but I am not calling the button live while the provider refuses the result. The derivative on this page was therefore finished deterministically with local ffmpeg from the same saved takes, saved sound and slate; the command and hashes are recorded in the studio. It is 720×1280, SAR 1:1, DAR 9:16, 17.79 seconds, with 44.1 kHz stereo audio. The earlier 4:5 file remains as evidence of the bug, not as the 9:16 proof.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={storyboard} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Assemble tab showing a saved storyboard with three shots and an audio bed, a render button, and the assembled result below." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The composition and its renders in one place. The edit controls and the results are the same application, the same project, the same stored media.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="figure">
