  <section id="change" class="prose">
    <h2>Changing the application</h2>
    <p>A studio you cannot change is just another subscription with extra steps. So after the first release I made a change to the same application, with the same projects and media still in place. Two things were wrong in ways a screenshot would not reveal.</p>
    <p><strong>The Assemble tab could not see saved storyboards.</strong> The server returned them; the client loader dropped the field. The tab said "No storyboard saved yet" while a storyboard with a rendered master existed — exactly the kind of defect that only appears when you look at the real page.</p>
    <p><strong>The audio length was fixed at thirty seconds.</strong> You could generate a bed, but not one that fit a seventeen-second cut. The container ended up thirty seconds long with a mostly silent tail.</p>
    <figure class="figure pair">
      <div>
        <img src={beforeEdit} width="1440" height="991" alt="Before: the Assemble tab says no storyboard saved yet, and the audio bed has no length choice." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Before: the saved storyboard missing from the tab.</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div>
        <img src={afterEdit} width="1440" height="900" alt="After: the same tab shows the saved storyboard, an editing notice, a changed-since-render note and two preserved renders." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>After: the same tab, with the composition loaded for editing and both renders kept.</figcaption>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <p>The change shipped as one release:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sound length:</strong> Studio Score takes 10, 15, 20, 25 or 30 seconds, and the estimate scales with the request.</li>
      <li><strong>Edit in place:</strong> "Edit shots" loads the existing storyboard; saving updates that composition rather than duplicating it.</li>
      <li><strong>Honest state:</strong> a cut that has changed since its last render says so, and earlier renders stay attached to it.</li>
      <li><strong>The loader fix:</strong> saved compositions survive the trip from the API to the tab.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Three smaller fixes came from the same evidence pass: assembled exports no longer appear in the shot picker (so a cut is never built from another cut), published media is briefly cacheable rather than cached for a day, and every published file, download and signed render input now answers byte-range requests with a real 206 and `Content-Range`, so a player can seek without downloading the whole file.</p>
    <p>The second change is the one the review asked for. The 9:16 label was wrong: the file was 4:5, and the portrait-first sort changed the edit to suit the frame. The label was removed first. The replacement composes a deliberate 1080×1920 slate, generates one 720×1280 vertical slate video with Wan 2.5, retains it as a reusable asset, then composes the selected takes in their chosen order after it with the saved sound. The job engine treats that as one generation row with two bounded provider stages, so the budget, retained bytes and tombstone rules are the same as every other render.</p>
    <p>The provider then changed underneath the fix. New `ffmpeg-api` compose jobs submit and report completed, but their result route returns 403 — "Access denied: You do not have permission to use this endpoint" — for the exact payload that worked earlier; the normal assembly path is blocked the same way. I also tested fal's documented direct call and a scheduled dispatch; neither completed reliably inside the studio's runtime, so the queue path stays and the honest state is a reconcilable permission block. I did not call the button fixed. The 9:16 derivative on this page was finished deterministically with local ffmpeg from the same saved takes, sound and slate: 720×1280, SAR 1:1, DAR 9:16, 17.79 seconds. The in-product path stays coded and unit-tested, and the article records the external permission block rather than hiding it in a limitations list.</p>
    <p>Before/after is measurable: the same storyboard still has the 17.02-second and 30.00-second masters in the library, both downloadable, neither regenerated. The failed 4:5 job and the failed provider compose jobs remain in history as the honest record of what was learned.</p>
    <p>The same release added the masked fill, cutout, upscale, Studio Effect, browser mix and the scoped Composer grants. Those are not a rewrite of the studio; they are the capabilities the earlier page said were missing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" class="prose">
    <h2>How it fits together</h2>
    <p>One diagram, no invented services. Composer is the creation and management surface; the studio is an independent application; Cloudflare runs both; the model vendors are called by the studio, with the studio's own keys.</p>
    <p>The selected sign-in improvement uses Cloudflare Access's email codes and passkeys, while agents keep their application keys. It needs no extra identity server. The existing Composer-to-studio handoff already avoids a second password; the account binding and live email/passkey rollout are still being completed. Our <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/docs/cloudflare-access-oyster.md">Cloudflare integration brief</a> explains the experience, prerequisites, Oyster contribution and optional Tailscale connection.</p>
    <div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: App Composer Init writes a specification and hands it to a supported agent. The agent deploys the Media Studio Worker with its own D1 database and R2 bucket. Composer's member grants name the current actor, workspace, selected project and read/edit/generate/publish/admin actions; a short-lived signed handoff opens the studio, which re-checks the current grant before a privileged dispatch. The studio calls Cloudflare Workers AI for stills and fal.ai for edits, video, sound and bounded renders, and serves public pages with byte ranges from its own origin.">
      <div class="node">
        <strong>App Composer</strong>
        <span>Get Ready · Init · saved brief · agent handoff · workspace/member grants · selected project · short-lived studio handoff</span>
      </div>
      <div class="arrow"><span>specification + pinned source</span></div>
      <div class="node">
        <strong>Your agent</strong>
        <span>creates the owner's copy · applies migrations · deploys · reads back the live revision</span>
      </div>
      <div class="arrow"><span>owner-controlled deployment</span></div>
      <div class="node wide">
        <strong>Media Studio Worker</strong>
        <span>projects · library · create/edit · sound and mix · storyboard · publish · job engine · scoped session</span>
        <div class="leaves">
          <span>D1: project, asset, job, budget, composition records</span>
          <span>R2: originals, retained results, assembled exports</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="arrow"><span>server-side calls, studio's own keys</span></div>
      <div class="node">
        <strong>Model vendors</strong>
        <span>Cloudflare Workers AI (stills) · fal.ai (masked edits, video, sound, bounded compose — the compose result route is currently blocked)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>The data flow for one assembly: a storyboard row stores the ordered take ids and the bed. The render job copies that manifest, signs a read link per participating asset, calls one compose request, then copies the resulting MP4 and its metadata into R2 and D1. Reopening the project reads those bytes; it does not call a model. The public page reads the same asset through a publication record, and withdrawal revokes that record.</p>
    <p>Composer's native "Images &amp; video" capability is a separate route for Composer's own applications: it calls an authorised Oyster installation's Prints app and records the job in Composer. Media Studio's routes are its own. Keeping them separate is deliberate — two job authorities for one studio would be worse than one honest boundary.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="evidence" class="prose">
    <h2>Evidence, costs and limits</h2>
    <p>Each advertised route was checked in four steps: the official documentation and schema, the actual account's access, a bounded request through the studio's real server adapter, and the released interface reading the retained output. The gaps are named, not smoothed over.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Qualification on 2026/11/13–2026/11/14, Australia/Melbourne (AEDT, UTC+11). "Live" means a real request through the studio and a saved result.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Route</th><th scope="col">Provider and model</th><th scope="col">State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Stills</th><td>Workers AI Lucid Origin, Phoenix, SDXL Lightning, FLUX.2 Klein</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Masked fill and outpaint</th><td>fal.ai FLUX.1 Fill, same-size mask, data-URI inputs</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Cutout and upscale</th><td>fal.ai BiRefNet v2 and Clarity Upscaler</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Video</th><td>fal.ai Hailuo 2.3 Standard, Kling 2.5 Turbo Pro</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Music</th><td>fal.ai Stable Audio</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Narration</th><td>ElevenLabs voice model hosted by fal.ai</td><td>Live via fal</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sound effect</th><td>ElevenLabs sound effects v2 hosted by fal.ai</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Narration (direct)</th><td>ElevenLabs official API</td><td>Not qualified: no account key held</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Assembly / in-product 9:16</th><td>fal.ai ffmpeg compose</td><td>Blocked: new jobs return 403 at the result route; earlier masters retained</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">9:16 derivative</th><td>Same saved takes, sound and slate; deterministic local ffmpeg finishing</td><td>Live: 720×1280, SAR 1:1, DAR 9:16, 17.79 s</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Scoped access</th><td>Composer session, workspace grant, project-scoped studio session, server re-check</td><td>Live on this release</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Direct ElevenLabs access is the one provider gap: the studio uses ElevenLabs' models through fal's hosted endpoint, billed by fal, and says so. ACE Music is excluded from this delivery entirely — no adapter, no key, no promise. The ffmpeg compose result block is an external permission change on the selected fal account; it is not a claim that the application or its stored work disappeared.</p>
    <h3>What it cost</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Selected request-level objects from both studio projects. Rows marked <code>est.</code> are planning estimates. None of these figures is invoice-confirmed; the vendor invoice remains authoritative, and the full ledger stays in the studio.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Object</th><th scope="col">Route</th><th scope="col">Cost</th><th scope="col">Outcome</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {costs.map(([name, route, cost, result]) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td><code>{route}</code></td><td>{cost}</td><td>{result}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
        <tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Recorded in the studio</th><td>both projects, all saved jobs</td><td>US$1.6647</td><td>US$1.3017 Paper &amp; Light + US$0.363 brand project; none invoice-confirmed</td></tr></tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Hosting is separate and shared: the studio runs on the same Workers Paid account as the rest of the estate, with R2 storage for originals and exports. Idle runtime does not bill per request, but the shared paid account and the stored bytes cost money whether or not anyone is rendering — there is no zero-cost idle state. The studio's own ledger records <strong>US$1.6647</strong> across the two projects: US$1.3017 in Paper &amp; Light and US$0.363 in the brand project. A further <strong>US$0.50</strong> qualified the 720p Wan slate video outside the studio budget; the rejected 1080p Wan attempt and the smaller LTX tests added roughly US$1.08 of qualification overhead. That is not a monthly price: it is what these specific requests cost. The saved narration asset is 23.25 seconds; earlier copy said 33 seconds, which was wrong, and the table now uses the asset record.</p>
    <h3>What is not claimed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>In-product compose is blocked.</strong> New fal ffmpeg compose jobs are accepted and reported complete, but the result route returns 403. The assembly and 9:16 controls stay coded; the 9:16 derivative here was finished deterministically with local ffmpeg, not by the blocked button.</li>
      <li><strong>Grants are workspace/installation level.</strong> The handoff binds one selected project and the studio enforces that scope, but People &amp; access does not yet offer a separate grant row per studio project. A wrong-project session is denied; per-project grants remain next work.</li>
      <li><strong>No voice cloning</strong>, no training on anyone's likeness, no rights workflow for either.</li>
      <li><strong>No full non-linear editor, no 4K, no 30-second single inference, no guarantee of identity across takes.</strong> The composition is a bounded manifest with a 60-second ceiling.</li>
      <li><strong>The mix is a browser mix.</strong> The controls and the offline WAV path exist; a complete music + narration + effect mix was exercised as a saved asset, not as a full studio export through the blocked compose route.</li>
      <li><strong>No by-ear listening review.</strong> Duration, loudness, transcript and waveform checks were performed; an audio-capable reviewer did not listen to the complete films or the generated mix in this run. That gate remains open.</li>
      <li><strong>No physical-device matrix.</strong> Phone, tablet and wide-desktop layout were checked in the browser at representative widths, including both themes and reduced motion; that is not a hardware claim.</li>
      <li><strong>The films are screen recordings</strong> with cuts and time compression stated in the captions; they are not one uninterrupted take, and the build/use/software-change recording is not yet a complete fresh-instance lesson.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="repeat" class="prose">
    <h2>Repeat it</h2>
    <p>You need a Cloudflare account on Workers Paid, a supported agent, and — for video, sound and rendering — a funded fal.ai key. Creating those accounts, signing in and approving spend are yours; the agent does the technical work. You do not need Docker, Wrangler or a database seed to start.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/init?start=media-studio">Build your studio</a>: choose Media Studio, name it, pick defaults, save the draft.</li>
      <li>Export the specification and copy the agent handoff. Check the pinned source and the human-only actions.</li>
      <li>Let your agent create your copy, apply the migrations, add the secrets and deploy. It should read back the live revision.</li>
      <li>Open the studio from the Composer menu. The entry names your workspace and selected project; read, edit, generate, publish and admin are separate grants. Upload something, make a still, apply a real mask, generate a bed or effect, mix the soundtrack, build a storyboard, publish one piece.</li>
      <li>Come back with a change you actually want. That is the part that makes it yours.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The <a href="/media-studio/tutorial">illustrated tutorial</a> carries the same recipe chapter by chapter, with what you do, what the agent does, what you should see, and how to recover. The <a href="/media-studio/media-studio-brief-20261114.json" download>portable brief used here</a> is a real exported example, not a sketch. The <a href="/media-studio/share-kit/README.txt">share kit</a> carries the public-safe text, alt text and derivatives from the brand project.</p>
    <p class="small">Source: <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/composer-media-studio">loftwah/composer-media-studio</a> (MIT) · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer">loftwah/app-composer</a>. Oyster, Prints and Darkroom are Levi's MIT-licensed Fungi projects. The studio keeps their attribution and licence notices.</p>
    <div class="row end">
      <a class="button" href="/init?start=media-studio">Start your own</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/media-studio/tutorial">Read the tutorial</a>
    </div>
  </section>
